<BaseLayout
	title="The 8 Points Hiding Behind a Third-Party Booking — Levent Can"
	description="How cross-referencing FCR, CSAT, and CES — instead of watching FCR alone — surfaced a single fixable process gap and a measured 5-point FCR gain."
>
	<section class="section prose">
		<a class="back" href="/work">← Work</a>
		<span class="tag">CASE STUDY</span>
		<h1>The 8 Points Hiding Behind a Third-Party Booking</h1>
		<p class="lede">
			FCR was missing target on a travel and tourism account. FCR alone couldn't say why —
			combining it with CSAT and CES could.
		</p>

		<h3>Problem</h3>
		<p>
			First Contact Resolution was running below target. Looked at on its own, the metric could
			only confirm that something was wrong — it gave no indication of the mechanism behind it,
			or what to actually do about it.
		</p>

		<h3>Data</h3>
		<p>FCR, CSAT, and CES at the individual contact level.</p>

		<h3>Analysis</h3>
		<p>
			Instead of treating FCR as a standalone number, I cross-referenced it against CSAT and CES
			and grouped contacts by combinations of the three — isolating, for example, cases where
			CSAT was strongly positive but FCR was still marked unresolved. That divergence was the
			signal: FCR on its own would never have surfaced it, since it can only tell you the score is
			off target, not why.
		</p>
		<p>
			Digging into that specific segment turned up a common thread: every one of those contacts
			involved a vacation booked through a third-party site. Agents had no visibility or edit
			access into those bookings, so the only option was to redirect the customer back to the
			third party — leaving the contact technically unresolved even when the customer was happy
			with the agent.
		</p>

		<h3>Solution</h3>
		<p>
			Brought the finding to the client with a specific, narrow ask: give agents visibility and
			edit access into third-party-booked vacations within the customer's account, closing the
			exact gap that was forcing a redirect instead of a resolution.
		</p>

		<h3>Result</h3>
		<p>
			Modeled an estimated 8-point FCR opportunity tied to this one process gap. By the time I
			moved to a different account, FCR here was up 5 points — a measured result, not just a
			projection, with the rest of the fix still rolling out.
		</p>
	</section>
</BaseLayout>

